  "name": "Hoyrat (Kirkuk Turkmen free-rhythm song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Short sung verse form of the Turkmen of Kirkuk, Tal Afar and other northern Iraqi towns, in which seven-syllable lines built on homonym rhymes (cinas) are delivered in long, unmetred melismatic phrases on a set of local melody types. A hoyrat is often sung alone or as the free prelude to a metred song with lute and drum; the oud and hand drum cards stand for that accompaniment.",
  "recipe": "classic modern hoyrat, ballad Poetry, med east poetic branch. neutral third half flat, many notes per syllable, solo with instrumental accompaniment. oud, hand drum source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
